<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-critically-damped" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Behavior of Critically Damped Solutions</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTRO + OBSERVATION: SOLUTION FORM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Finally, let's look at the borderline case sitting right between the previous
    two. When <m>c^2 = 4km</m>, the characteristic equation has one repeated real
    root <m>r = -c/(2m)</m>, and the system is called <term>critically damped</term>.
    The general solution takes the form:
    <me>x(t) = c_1 e^{rt} + c_2 t e^{rt}.</me>
    If this setup feels familiar, that's because it is, this page will closely
    mirror the overdamped page, just with a repeated root instead of two distinct ones.
  </p>

  <justification>
    <title>Why is the root negative? (can skim)</title>
    <p>
      The repeated root is <m>r = -c/(2m)</m>. Since <m>c > 0</m> and <m>m > 0</m>,
      the numerator is negative and the denominator is positive, so <m>r \lt 0</m>.
    </p>
  </justification>

  <observation xml:id="obs-critdamped-form">
    <title>Critically Damped Solution Form</title>
    <p>
      When <m>c^2 = 4km</m>, the characteristic equation has one repeated real root
      <m>r = -c/(2m) \lt 0</m>, and the system is <term>critically damped</term>.
      The general solution takes the form:
      <me>x(t) = c_1 e^{rt} + c_2 t e^{rt},</me>
      where <m>r \lt 0</m>.
    </p>
  </observation>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       PROSE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Like the overdamped case in <xref ref="sec-overdamped"/>, there are no
    trig functions anywhere in sight, so there is no oscillation. The new feature
    here is the <m>te^{rt}</m> term, which we saw arise from the repeated root
    structure in <xref ref="sec-all-three-cases"/>.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTERACTIVE 1: SPRING ANIMATION + x(t) GRAPH
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <figure xml:id="fig-critdamped-spring">
    <caption>
      A critically damped spring with <m>r = -0.5</m>, <m>c_1 = -1</m>,
      <m>c_2 = -2</m>, so <m>x(t) = -e^{-0.5t} - 2te^{-0.5t}</m>.
      Press Play and notice the mass starts compressed, becomes
      more compressed before turning around, then slowly approaches equilibrium
      without ever crossing it.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-critdamped-spring"
      iframe="ch3-graph/interactive-critdamped-spring.html"
      width="100%" aspect="5:2">
      <description>
        On the left, an animation of a critically damped mass on a spring and dashpot.
        The mass starts compressed, moves further into compression, then turns around
        and slowly approaches equilibrium from the compressed side without crossing it.
        On the right, the corresponding graph of x of t is traced in real time.
      </description>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       PROSE: RAISE THE EQUILIBRIUM QUESTION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    In the animation above, the mass never crossed equilibrium. But just like the
    overdamped case, whether or not it crosses depends on <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m>.
    Play around with the explorer below and see what you notice.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTERACTIVE 2: EXPLORER (GRAPH ONLY, c1/c2 SLIDERS)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <figure xml:id="fig-critdamped-explore">
    <caption>
      The critically damped solution <m>x(t) = c_1 e^{-0.5t} + c_2 t e^{-0.5t}</m>
      with <m>r = -0.5</m> fixed.
      Drag the sliders to change <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m> and observe how many
      times the curve crosses <m>x = 0</m>.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-critdamped-explore"
      iframe="ch3-graph/interactive-critdamped-explore.html"
      width="100%" aspect="5:2">
      <description>
        A graph of the critically damped solution with sliders for c1 and c2.
        The equation display updates in real time to show the current function.
        The graph updates immediately as sliders move and a message reports
        how many times the curve crosses x equals 0. Students discover that
        the curve crosses at most once regardless of c1 and c2.
      </description>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       MC CHECKPOINT
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mc-critdamped-crossings">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Based on your exploration above, what are the possibilities for how many
        times the mass passes through the equilibrium position <m>x = 0</m> in
        a critically damped system?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>Never or exactly once.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! Just like the overdamped case, the mass passes through equilibrium
            at most once. <m>\checkmark</m>
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Exactly once, always.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not always. Try <m>c_1 = 1</m> and <m>c_2 = 1</m> in the explorer above.
            The curve never crosses <m>x = 0</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Infinitely many times.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            That is the underdamped case. Critically damped systems have no oscillation.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Exactly twice.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Try as many values of <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m> as you like in the
            explorer. You will not find a case where the curve crosses twice.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXAMPLE: ALGEBRAIC PROOF OF AT MOST ONCE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    The explorer suggests the mass crosses equilibrium at most once, no matter what
    <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m> are. Let's confirm this by digging into the algebra,
    and figure out the long-term behavior along the way.
  </p>

  <example xml:id="ex-critdamped-crossings-algebra">
    <title>At Most One Equilibrium Crossing</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Show that the mass in a critically damped system passes through the equilibrium
        position at most one time. What is the long-term behavior?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We want to find all <m>t \geq 0</m> where <m>x(t) = 0</m>. Setting the
        general solution equal to zero:
        <me>c_1 e^{rt} + c_2 t e^{rt} = 0.</me>
        Since <m>e^{rt} \neq 0</m> for all <m>t</m>, we can divide both sides by
        <m>e^{rt}</m>:
        <me>c_1 + c_2 t = 0,</me>
        so
        <me>t = -\frac{c_1}{c_2}.</me>
        This is at most one value of <m>t</m>. It gives a valid crossing only when
        <m>-c_1/c_2 \geq 0</m>. Otherwise the mass never crosses equilibrium at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the long-term behavior, since <m>r \lt 0</m>:
        <me>
          \lim_{t \to \infty} x(t)
          = \lim_{t \to \infty}
            \underbrace{e^{rt}}_{\to\, 0}
            \cdot
            \underbrace{(c_1 + c_2 t)}_{\text{grows}}
          = 0,
        </me>
        where the exponential decay beats the linear growth by L'Hopital's rule.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       OBSERVATION: LONG TERM BEHAVIOR
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    So at most one crossing along the way, but where does the mass end up after all
    that?
  </p>

  <observation xml:id="obs-critdamped-limit">
    <p>
      Even though <m>c_1 + c_2 t</m> grows without bound, the exponential decay
      wins:
      <me>
        \lim_{t \to \infty} x(t)
        = \lim_{t \to \infty}
          \underbrace{(c_1 + c_2 t)}_{\text{grows}}
          \cdot
          \underbrace{e^{rt}}_{\to\, 0}
        = 0.
      </me>
      The mass always approaches equilibrium in the long run.
    </p>
  </observation>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Solution form:</alert> Critically damped solutions have the form
          <m>x(t) = c_1 e^{rt} + c_2 t e^{rt}</m> where <m>r \lt 0</m> is the
          one repeated root.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>No oscillation:</alert> Like the overdamped case, there is no
          trigonometric part and therefore no oscillation.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Equilibrium crossings:</alert> The mass passes through <m>x = 0</m>
          at most once, the same as the overdamped case.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Long-term behavior:</alert>
          <m>\displaystyle\lim_{t \to \infty} x(t) = 0</m>,
          since the exponential decay beats the linear growth in <m>c_1 + c_2 t</m>.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Similarity to overdamped:</alert> Critically damped and overdamped
          solutions behave qualitatively the same way: no oscillation, at most one
          equilibrium crossing, and <m>x(t) \to 0</m>. The difference is in the
          form of the solution and which value of <m>c</m> produces each case.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       VIDEO + QUESTION FORM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

